% ============================================================================
% phoneme-blending — combining separate sounds to say a word, a NINTH
% literacy sub-skill in the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Deliberately DIFFERENT in shape from all eight prior literacy libraries:
% `word-families.adj` derives RHYMING, `syllable-count.adj` recalls a
% SYLLABLE COUNT, `initial-sound.adj` recalls a BEGINNING sound,
% `onset-rime.adj` recalls an ONSET/RIME split, `phoneme-substitution.adj`
% recalls the word formed by SWAPPING one sound for another,
% `phoneme-deletion.adj` recalls the word formed by REMOVING one sound, and
% `syllable-deletion.adj`/`syllable-substitution.adj` do the same at the
% SYLLABLE level. This library goes the OPPOSITE direction from deletion and
% substitution: it recalls the word formed by BLENDING three separate sounds
% together, grounding the same phonemic-awareness continuum's blending skill
% as a pure lookup: `phoneme_blending(sound_one, sound_two, sound_three,
% word)`. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? phoneme_blending(s, o, p, $Word)   % soap
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a FOUR-column table like
% `phoneme-substitution.adj`): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `phoneme_blending(sound_one, sound_two, sound_three, word)` carrying the
% Reading Rockets citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the blended word WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on any sound triple not the one the
% cited page walks through. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind the word and
% recall the three sounds that blend into it:
%
%     ? phoneme_blending($S1, $S2, $S3, soap)   % s, o, p
%
% The one blend this table carries, as a little truth table:
%
%     sound_one   sound_two   sound_three   word
%     ---------   ---------   -----------   ----
%     s           o           p             soap
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Phonological and
% Phonemic Awareness: In Practice" module — the SAME already-vetted page
% `syllable-count.adj`, `phoneme-substitution.adj`, `phoneme-deletion.adj`,
% `syllable-substitution.adj`, and `syllable-deletion.adj` all already cite,
% a different section of it (its "Blending sounds" demonstration) —
% WebFetch-verified THREE separate times for consistency before writing, all
% byte-identical. The exact span (one sentence, stating both the three sounds
% and the word they blend into):
%
%   "We can blend sounds to say a word. Watch me. The sounds are /s/ [slide a
%    cap], /ō/ [slide a cap], /p/ [slide a cap]. I will touch and say, then
%    blend. /s/ [touch first cap], /ō/ [touch middle cap], /p/ [touch last
%    cap], soap [sweeping a finger below the caps]."
%
% `trust consensus` — the same tier this page's sibling sections already
% earned. Deliberately scoped to ONLY this ONE blend the cited page walks
% through step by step — honestly narrow (mirroring `phoneme-deletion.adj`'s
% and `phoneme-substitution.adj`'s precedent of one clean row) rather than
% inventing a blend the source does not demonstrate. This same page also has
% an "Adding sounds" section (/ī/ + /s/ -> "ice") that blends only TWO sounds
% rather than three — a distinct two-column arity, left as a future
% phoneme-addition.adj candidate rather than force-fit into this table. A
% future slice can add a second blend to this table once a comparably clean
% three-sound citation is found. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table phoneme_blending {
    columns sound_one, sound_two, sound_three, word

    row (s, o, p, soap)

    source "We can blend sounds to say a word. Watch me. The sounds are /s/ [slide a cap], /ō/ [slide a cap], /p/ [slide a cap]. I will touch and say, then blend. /s/ [touch first cap], /ō/ [touch middle cap], /p/ [touch last cap], soap [sweeping a finger below the caps]."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice"
    trust consensus
}
